 bar 33 (Gb4 eighths repeated, then the Db5 rf). The last two bars each hold two half notes with tremolo strokes on the same pitch. These are the repeated and tremolo A4s of bars 34 and 35. The line ends at the barline after bar 35. That makes 9 bars, 27 through 35. No bar is missing and none is extra. Pitches in the abbreviated bars 32–35 are hard to read exactly. Their rhythm and the barline count still agree.","right":true,"insights":"The opening is easy to match: the half rest and the dolce figure Bb–Eb–D–C, repeated in bar 29, fix bar 27 for certain. The second half is harder because the copyist writes the repeated eighths and tremolos as stroked half notes. Counting barlines settles it. Showing the edition's repeated eighths as abbreviations would make this easier to decide."},"ttft_ms":15003,"type":"result","duration_ms":39687,"uuid":"aeace571-4ec0-4eff-91cd-57b0459cb3a6","ttft_stream_ms":1679,"time_to_request_ms":370,"first_content_frame_ms":1679,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"model_usage_since_last_result":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inputTokens":4,"outputTokens":3466,"cacheReadInputTokens":1046,"cacheCreationInputTokens":11791,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.1638732,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":2731,"cacheCreationEphemeral5mInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationEphemeral1hInputTokens":11791,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}}}
